% Goal

Implement and maintain `brain/order_constraints.py` as the The riskiest module in the feature: the only one that changes behaviour inside working, ear-tested ordering code. CONTRACT BUNCHES INTO SUPERNODES, DO NOT PENALIZE EDGES. The textbook clustered-TSP reduction adds a large constant M to inter-cluster edges, but that guarantee holds only for an EXACT solver; greedy_mix_order is a nearest-neighbour tour, so a greedy pass can strand a member no matter how big M is. contract(rows, groups) collapses each n-ary group to one pseudo-track whose entry features come from the first member and exit features from the last, which makes non-contiguity structurally impossible AND fixes the real defect: the seams finally get scored, because the in-edge scores against the head and the out-edge against the tail. expand(order, table) restores members and NO PSEUDO ID MAY APPEAR IN ANY RETURNED ORDER. assert_intact raises on non-contiguity as a post-condition check, which the current code has none of. merge_groups(pairs) promotes pairwise constraints to n-ary groups so chaining (A,B) then (B,C) yields [A,B,C] and DOES NOT STRAND A, the verified defect at mix_order_brief.py:199 where both members are pulled out and reinserted. Replaces the pairwise implementation behind the existing dict shape so mix_order_brief's callers are unchanged.

